[[:db/retract 17592186125059 :question/answer-fields 17592186122954] [:db/retract 17592186125054 :question/answer-fields 17592186122924] {:db/id 17592186124360 :question/problem "![](images/43/439695c5cc3c9500616e1b847b74fc873c3a126cb2e8b6334b52d968def29e0b.svg)\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-0) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=4$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{1}$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{1}) = 1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 4.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{4}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-4I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda_2 = 4.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{1}) + \\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 1+2 = 3. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n![](images/26/26d2a7183e05b32e46c8f0e9e8a395936b45f84b949d61e67f6a01c1dca2ea4a.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124344 :question/problem "![](images/db/dbad3d052beb54ca79ea66a4ec20ed9cb4f226fbc9d5c53dbb9a2a2707adf387.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PDP^{-1},$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D=P^{-1}AP$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_3=-2 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-9.$\n\nThe second column of $P$ corresponds to $\\lambda=-2.$ Computing $A-\\lambda I,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -8\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1 = -x_2+2x_3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n![](images/6b/6be8b26caa9f7c3eb3f467cfc0ed237a0576799a6ae5c620110c82c99ad8f58a.svg)\n\nComparing this with the second column of $P,$ we deduce that $x_2=0.$ Now, setting $x_3=1$ and substituting our values for $x_2$ and $x_3$ into the general solution, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{2}{1}=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124410 :question/problem "![](images/ef/efded60c85aa3a7bb1ec21e159904983da2fe6216520f628109eff0d0e6fb1f5.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PDP^{-1},$ what is the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A=PDP^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_3=0.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=4.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2+3x_3.$ Hence, the general solution is\n![](images/d0/d035dcaecd0c84608677f292c7e9b1acd0efc1a5bdd1d0ac43b3a9ba3808295f.svg)\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/b7/b7079fa1df34dd45f1899f99f83bb4c4d45a378a86a55827cad09355779ca011.svg) and ![](images/fa/fab597dd2894f23ba307ac5dfd631560b02914757454c42f90b27d10ecd85349.svg)\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/a2/a2e8e64f0907a900b1078ced3042ebdaade46cdcee461ece7194d16cfb4b4c20.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n![](images/1c/1ca56d30833ee1641678bb74c93dba72b459112a0bde385875a958847bc61c6f.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125793 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/ea/ea31a54b90a066b34d28d1af82ee31515f06622a873d5790b2dd73cb2bbd8bb8.svg)\n\nThis is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, yet it has only *two* distinct eigenvalues, namely $\\lambda_1 = 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = 1.$ Note that $\\lambda_1$ is a simple root of the  characteristic polynomial and $\\lambda_2$ is a double root.\n\nTo diagonalize a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we need to find a basis of $\\mathbb R^3$ consisting of linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$ So the question is, can we diagonalize this matrix even though it does not have three distinct eigenvalues?\n\nLet's start by computing the eigenvectors of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 0.$ Solving the equation $A \\mathbf x = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf x$ in the usual way, we obtain a single linearly independent eigenvector:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 1.$ Let's solve the equation $A \\mathbf x = \\lambda_2 \\mathbf x$ by reducing $(A - \\lambda_2 I)$ to row-echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is one pivot column in the reduced matrix above (the $3$rd one). Thus, $x_1$ and $x_2$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain the solution $x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/1b/1bfc4a70075d48f926789a8a1d531b20b060172f2eebe862ee5e1a34ed315688.svg)\nwhere $x_1, x_2\\in \\mathbb R.$ Thus, we have obtained *two* linearly independent eigenvectors:\n![](images/a5/a513d529bf797d62ca4df57b37adc3c57e719db4773165b744c70b5a8e071dda.svg)\n\nSo $A$ has three linearly independent eigenvectors that form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Therefore, $A$ is diagonalizable! \n\nIn terms of eigenspaces, $A$ is diagonalizable because the sum of the dimensions of the eigenspaces equals the number of rows/columns of $A.$ The eigenspaces corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = 1$ are given by\n\n![](images/6e/6e0e00d51cd3eaf3ad0f5d2f7d07d4bd0c00e80d9fc66ccebc690671e98850b8.svg) \n\nIf we sum the dimensions of $V_0$ and $V_1,$ we get precisely $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dim(V_{0}) + \\dim(V_{1}) = 1 + 2 = 3 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nIt's important to realize that not all matrices are diagonalizable! Let's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125856 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/db/dba80d814848486260c88ce625464f75a18e09963dfd7e16603462cb857b302c.svg)\n\nIts eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=2$ (double root).\n\nLet's see how many linearly independent eigenvectors we can find for this matrix.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 1.$ Solving the equation $A \\mathbf x = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf x$ in the usual way, we obtain a single linearly independent eigenvector:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 2.$ Let's solve the equation $A \\mathbf x = \\lambda_2 \\mathbf x$ by reducing $(A - \\lambda_2 I)$ to row-echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we have\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_3 = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n![](images/f1/f1b718cdc6cbb49c9cad5faada737abddccadc83f6f1e94ae64d096863a304ef.svg)\nwhere $x_2\\in\\mathbb R.$ Thus, we have obtained *only one* linearly independent eigenvector:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo $A$ has *only two* linearly independent eigenvectors, and these cannot form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Therefore, $A$ is *not* diagonalizable.\n\nIn terms of eigenspaces, the matrix $A$ is not diagonalizable because the sum of the dimensions of the eigenspaces is *smaller* than the number of rows/columns of $A.$ The eigenspaces corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 1$ and $\\lambda_2 = 2$ are given by\n\n![](images/93/93a38c26711366b4a091900bd410648137a5fede4a155b411038dc3a82a19e23.svg) \n\nIf we sum the dimensions of $V_1$ and $V_2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dim(V_{1}) + \\dim(V_{2}) = 1 + 1 = 2 \\neq 3 \\quad {\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol\\times}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $A$ is *not* diagonalizable." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125862 :tutorial/content "To check whether diagonalization is possible, we can use the following theorem:\n\n*An $n \\times n$ matrix is diagonalizable if and only if the sum of the dimensions of the eigenspaces equals $n$.*\n\nThat is, if \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1, \\: \\lambda_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\lambda_k\n$$\n\nare the distinct eigenvalues of an $n \\times n$ matrix, and the corresponding eigenspaces are \n\n$$\nV_{\\lambda_1}, \\: V_{\\lambda_2}, \\: \\ldots, \\: V_{\\lambda_k}\n$$\n\nthen the matrix is diagonalizable if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dim(V_{\\lambda_1}) + \\dim(V_{\\lambda_2}) + \\cdots + \\dim(V_{\\lambda_k}) = n.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125857 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is diagonalizable if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA = PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSo, diagonalizing $A$ means finding matrices $D$ and $P$ that satisfy this equation. \n\nFor example, let's diagonalize the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/da/da4b2dd12205b20515a36d06bc5de5b577bec362c5eb9f7942e795fe1511acfb.svg)\n\nTo diagonalize a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, we follow a three-step **diagonalization algorithm.**\n\n**Step 1:** Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.\n\nWe solve the characteristic equation $\\vert A - \\lambda I \\vert = 0.$ For the given matrix, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\lambda_1}} = {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2}} = {\\color{red}2}\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad{\\color{blue}\\lambda_3 =1}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Find a basis (if it exists) consisting of eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFollowing the usual method for computing eigenvectors, we get \n\n![](images/57/572a2da243557642332de657b41ee28237385eeaa59ebcef747dd73f9d8e4c36.svg)\n\ncorresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\color{red}{\\lambda_1}} = {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2}} = {\\color{red}2}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_3 =1},$ respectively.\n\n**Watch out!** In this example, the total number of linearly independent eigenvectors equals the number of rows/columns of the matrix. If we obtain fewer eigenvectors at this step, then the matrix is *not* diagonalizable.\n\n**Step 3: ** Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNow that we have our eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors, we can construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and invertible matrix $P.$\n\nThe matrix $D$ has the eigenvalues of the matrix as its entries on the main diagonal. We can write them in any order, so we let\n\n![](images/45/45b4df46c058d00fd8186b2f9dc83ba9772bbe88b17866e6d188ba340e890d6d.svg)\n\nThen, the columns of $P$ are the corresponding eigenvectors. We must write each eigenvector in the *same* column as its respective eigenvalue in the matrix $D.$ So, we get\n\n![](images/c6/c64a8e7a572d5b6dce58390644d5f79d905fcf22c8427487dcc375bc851f7b12.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=PD{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** When diagonalizing a diagonalizable matrix, we can place the eigenvalues into the matrix $P$ in any order. We just need to be sure that the diagonal entries in the matrix $D$ correspond to the columns of $P.$\n\nFor example:\n\n- Swapping the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ we get another valid diagonalization: \n![](images/60/6026d7be0662360726a109c6c665d59a10b805303b2b71934352688c2f17b357.svg)\n\n- Swapping the columns $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ above gives another valid diagonalization: \n![](images/22/223f68ef8ba143f1766224972d11e1b899afb64bd8828ec95d7b1c5a2d95519e.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124756 